1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,017
Hello! My name is Madhavan. Today I will show you my project, EventHub.

2
00:00:06,467 --> 00:00:10,783
EventHub is a website that runs all of a college's events in one place.

3
00:00:11,233 --> 00:00:15,294
I built it with React, Spring Boot and MySQL.

4
00:00:15,744 --> 00:00:20,133
Today, many colleges run events on WhatsApp groups and Google Forms.

5
00:00:20,583 --> 00:00:28,001
This causes problems: two people booking the last seat, shared ticket screenshots and late certificates.

6
00:00:28,451 --> 00:00:32,986
EventHub solves each of these problems with a rule on the server.

7
00:00:33,436 --> 00:00:39,869
There are five kinds of users: visitor, student, organizer, volunteer and admin.

8
00:00:40,319 --> 00:00:43,373
The server checks the user's role on every request.

9
00:00:43,823 --> 00:00:48,877
The React app runs in the browser. It talks to the Spring Boot server using JSON.

10
00:00:49,327 --> 00:00:55,498
Each request goes through the security filters, the controller, the service and the repository to reach MySQL.

11
00:00:55,948 --> 00:01:01,551
This is the home page. A student can search for an event or click a tag.

12
00:01:02,001 --> 00:01:06,516
The event page shows the price and how many seats are left, live.

13
00:01:06,966 --> 00:01:10,306
Ravi chooses one ticket and presses Book now.

14
00:01:10,756 --> 00:01:15,098
While he pays, the seat is kept for him for ten minutes.

15
00:01:15,548 --> 00:01:23,396
Even if a hundred people book at the same moment, no extra seats are sold. Optimistic locking guarantees this.

16
00:01:23,846 --> 00:01:28,009
In development this is a test payment page. No real money is used.

17
00:01:28,459 --> 00:01:31,951
With a Stripe key, Stripe's real checkout page appears instead.

18
00:01:32,401 --> 00:01:37,760
After paying, Ravi gets a QR ticket. An email arrives too.

19
00:01:38,210 --> 00:01:43,749
The QR code contains only a random ticket code. No private information.

20
00:01:44,199 --> 00:01:47,803
On the event day, volunteer Priya scans the ticket at the gate.

21
00:01:48,253 --> 00:01:50,736
First scan: a green LET IN.

22
00:01:51,186 --> 00:01:59,099
Scanned again, the same ticket shows a red ALREADY USED, with when and by whom.

23
00:01:59,549 --> 00:02:07,963
This works with a single SQL UPDATE. Even if twenty gates scan at once, only one entry is allowed.

24
00:02:08,413 --> 00:02:13,038
When the event is over, only people who really came get a PDF certificate.

25
00:02:13,488 --> 00:02:18,982
Any company can type the certificate number on this page and check that it is genuine.

26
00:02:19,432 --> 00:02:23,946
Now organizer Madhavan creates a new event for the Coding Club.

27
00:02:24,396 --> 00:02:26,667
Both the browser and the server check every field.

28
00:02:27,117 --> 00:02:33,575
Students see the event only after the admin approves it. Every change is recorded in the audit log.

29
00:02:34,025 --> 00:02:40,609
Organizers have an analytics dashboard: tickets and money per day, check-in rate and rating.

30
00:02:41,059 --> 00:02:46,747
The database does the counting, and the answer is cached for sixty seconds.

31
00:02:47,197 --> 00:02:52,563
When Kavya, an organizer of another club, tries to open the admin page, she is refused.

32
00:02:53,013 --> 00:02:59,838
Passwords are protected with BCrypt, and the login token is kept in an httpOnly cookie.

33
00:03:00,288 --> 00:03:07,398
The project has 146 automated tests. Service code coverage is 91 percent.

34
00:03:07,848 --> 00:03:11,299
GitHub Actions runs all the tests on every push.

35
00:03:11,749 --> 00:03:18,007
In short, EventHub is a complete, secure and well-tested full stack project.

36
00:03:18,457 --> 00:03:22,466
The next step is to deploy it on the internet. Thank you!
